Subject: d2d1: blend ClearType glyph runs per subpixel channel

Patch 0081 gave DirectWrite's ClearType textures real subpixel coverage,
one sample per channel. d2d1 then threw it away: it wrapped the texture
in an A8 bitmap three times as wide, made a bitmap brush of it, and
filled the run rectangle with that brush as an opacity mask. Three
samples spanning one pixel are filtered back into one coverage value, so
the result was the greyscale the mask was meant to improve on, and text
drawn through Direct2D looked the same either way. That is the path
every Direct2D application takes.

Draw the run once per channel instead. Each pass is masked by that
channel's coverage alone and writes only its own colour channel, which
gives each channel the same premultiplied-over blend it would have had
by itself: dst.r = brush.r * cov.r + dst.r * (1 - cov.r), and likewise
for green and blue. Three blend states, cached on the device context and
differing from the standard one only in the write mask, do the
restriction; the shape pipeline and the shader are untouched.

Coverage has to reach the target's alpha channel exactly once rather
than three times, so alpha rides with the green pass, green being the
channel closest to perceived luminance.

The cost is three draw calls per glyph run where there was one. Runs
that are not ClearType keep the single-pass path exactly as before.

